# Reference template for a dubIS /v1 server on a Unix socket that
# `tailscale serve` proxies into — the shape on `600g4` (aspen).
#
# NOT applied by anything in this repo, and NOT installed on any box by the
# change that added this file. Copy it to the box, fill in the CHANGE-ME, and
# point the unit at it with:
#
#     [Service]
#     EnvironmentFile=/etc/dubis/tailnet-serve.env
#
# Full background, the trust boundary, and the verification recipe:
# docs/tailnet-socket-proxy.md.
#
# WHY these four lines exist: when a proxy fronts the socket, the kernel peer
# is the proxy (`tailscaled`, uid 0), so `SO_PEERCRED` names the wrong party and
# every tailnet caller's mutations are stamped `@root`. `DUBIS_UDS_PROXY_UIDS`
# is what lets the operator say "that uid is a hop, not a person". Without it
# nothing changes anywhere — there is no built-in "uid 0 is a proxy" rule.

# Per-user identity needs the middleware installed. With `off` every caller is
# `local` and this file does nothing at all.
DUBIS_AUTH_MODE=on

# The master switch for believing ANY Tailscale-User-Login header. The socket
# hop is gated on it too: naming a proxy uid is not the same as opting into
# header trust.
DUBIS_TRUST_TAILSCALE_HEADER=1

# Every login this server will ever admit through the hop. This is a bound on
# WHO may be named, not the thing that makes the header trustworthy — see the
# "who can forge this" section of the doc. Comma-separated, no spaces.
DUBIS_TAILNET_ALLOWLIST=CHANGE-ME@example.com

# The proxy's uid, as the kernel reports it for the socket peer. `0` because
# tailscaled runs as root. A name works too (`tailscale`), resolved through the
# passwd file at startup; an entry that is neither a number nor a known account
# is a startup failure, never a silently dropped entry.
#
# Leave this line out entirely to keep today's behavior exactly: every
# Unix-socket caller resolves to its own peer uid, `root` included.
DUBIS_UDS_PROXY_UIDS=0

# The assumption this file makes and the server cannot check: that tailscaled
# injects Tailscale-User-Login from an AUTHENTICATED tailnet session and owns
# the header. On `tailscale serve` that means the handler requires tailnet auth
# (`tailscale serve --auth`), so an unauthenticated request never reaches the
# socket. Prove it before enabling — the doc's step 4 is exactly that check.
